    {
      "id": "p0201-b011",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "A spider monster named Muffet appears in Hotland. In English, Muffet tends to call the main character \"dearie\", but not in Japanese. This is because the sentiment is already conveyed by her cordial, feminine speech pattern in Japanese - including the use of the polite, feminine pronoun watashi.",
        "raw": "A spider monster named Muffet appears in Hotland. In English, Muffet tends\nto call the main character \"dearie\", but not in Japanese. This is because the\nsentiment is already conveyed by her cordial, feminine speech pattern in\nJapanese - including the use of the polite, feminine pronoun watashi.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.09697121,
          0.70762857,
          0.68772949,
          0.77537857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.09446158,
          0.70691429,
          0.70748138,
          0.77645
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          62,
          63,
          64,
          65
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "热域里会出现蜘蛛怪物 Muffet。英语里，她常把主角叫作“dearie”（亲爱的），日语却不会这样叫，因为亲切、女性化的说话方式已经传达了这种感觉，其中还包括用礼貌、女性化的 watashi（我）自称。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
          {
            "start": 89,
            "end": 96,
            "italic": true,
            "lang": "en"
          }
        ]
