    {
      "id": "p0201-b012",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "In English, Muffet uses tildes (the squiggly - mark) at the end of words to denote a cute, upbeat tone of voice. This use of tildes is still uncommon in English, which lends Muffet a distinctive vibe. Tilde-like characters are extremely common in Japanese entertainment, so Muffet retains her squiggles in the Japanese script. Basically, this tilde use is a good example of how Toby blended certain Japanese language quirks into Undertale's original English script.",
        "raw": "In English, Muffet uses tildes (the squiggly - mark) at the end of words to denote\na cute, upbeat tone of voice. This use of tildes is still uncommon in English,\nwhich lends Muffet a distinctive vibe. Tilde-like characters are extremely\ncommon in Japanese entertainment, so Muffet retains her squiggles in the\nJapanese script. Basically, this tilde use is a good example of how Toby blended\ncertain Japanese language quirks into Undertale's original English script.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.09738779,
          0.79027143,
          0.7054493,
          0.89464286
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.09446158,
          0.78955714,
          0.70748138,
          0.89571429
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          66,
          67,
          68,
          69,
          70,
          71
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "在英语中，Muffet 会在词尾加波浪号“~”，表现可爱、轻快的语气。英语里这种用法仍不常见，因此显得她很有特色。类似波浪号的符号在日本娱乐作品中却极为普遍，所以日文保留了这些弯弯的符号。这也很好地说明，Toby 如何把日语的一些特点融进《Undertale》的英语原剧本。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
          {
